# Cloudflare reads this from the build output (like public/_redirects) — from dist/ on the
# Pages app, and from build/ on the design Worker.
#
# Everything Vite emits under /assets/ is content-hashed, so the long immutable cache is
# safe — a change ships under a new URL. `embed.js` deliberately sits outside this rule at
# the dist root: it is the one stable URL hosts hardcode, and it must stay revalidated.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two unhashed entry files at the dist root (#148). Everything above is
# content-hashed and so may be cached forever; these carry stable names precisely so hosts
# can hardcode them, which means the CDN must revalidate instead.
#
# **This rule is a fix, not a formality.** The file's previous version left `embed.js`
# outside `/assets/*` and said it "must stay revalidated" — but nothing enforced it, so the
# platform default applied, and the default differs per host: `sahajatlas.com` served
# `max-age=14400` while `*.pages.dev` served `max-age=0`. Four hours of freshness on the one
# unhashed file that imports content-hashed chunks is exactly the failure `docs/embedding.md`
# warns hosts about — a stale loader asking for chunk names the deploy no longer has, which
# kills the widget with no fallback — and it was invisible on the host every check runs
# against.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` rather than `no-store`: a conditional request that answers 304
# is cheap, and it keeps the browser cache useful while making staleness impossible. CORS
# reaches these two through the site-wide rule at the bottom, which is the only place it may
# be set — see that rule for why.
/auto.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/embed.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate


# The site-wide rule, and the ONLY place Access-Control-Allow-Origin may be set.
#
# CORS, first. The widget is embedded in OTHER sites, so everything it loads at runtime is a
# cross-origin request from the host page's point of view. Fonts are the strict case: a font
# is always fetched in CORS mode, so a missing Access-Control-Allow-Origin means the
# self-hosted Raleway silently fails to render (issue #91). A `type="module"` script is
# fetched in CORS mode too, which is why the two unhashed entry files need it as well.
#
# This is a RENDERING REQUIREMENT, not a nicety, and it is stated here because the platform
# default differs by platform. Cloudflare Pages adds `access-control-allow-origin: *` to
# every static asset by itself; Workers static assets add none — their only defaults are
# Content-Type, Cache-Control, ETag and CF-Cache-Status. So on the Pages app this rule
# changes no observable behaviour, and on the design Worker it is the whole of CORS.
# sydevs/SahajCloud#913 depends on a library's assets staying readable cross-origin.
#
# ⚠ EXACTLY ONE ACAO rule may match any given path. Both platforms apply EVERY matching rule
# and join repeated values with a comma, so a second ACAO rule anywhere above would serve
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, *` on /assets/*, /auto.js and /embed.js — an invalid value
# that re-opens #91. `tests/smoke/cache-headers.smoke.test.ts` asserts the value is exactly
# `*`, so a comma-joined one fails a live spec rather than shipping. This is why the three
# rules above carry Cache-Control only.
#
# Indexing, second (issue #106). The policy and the robots.txt interaction are written down
# once, in public/robots.txt; what follows is only why this half exists.
#
# A HEADER reaches what a <meta> cannot. index.html's tag covers every ROUTE (`_redirects`
# rewrites unknown paths to that shell), but the three root entry files and the hashed assets
# are each a fetchable URL with no document to carry a tag. This covers all of them.
#
# It cannot leak into a host page's own indexing: X-Robots-Tag governs the URL it is served
# on, and a directive on a subresource says nothing about the document embedding it.
# `noindex` alone, deliberately — no `nofollow`, because the canonicals point at WeMeditate
# and there is no reason to stop a crawler following them there.
#
# Ordered last for reading only: this rule and the three above set disjoint header NAMES.
/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
